{
"dur": 70.943,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.28,
"text": "By the early 1970s, Blue Ribbon and Onitsuka didn't trust each other. Each suspected the other was about to cut it out.",
"end": 7.555
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.555,
"vo_start": 7.83,
"vo_end": 16.589,
"text": "Knight needed his own brand, fast. For a logo, he asked Carolyn Davidson, a design student he'd met at Portland State, for something that suggested motion.",
"end": 16.864
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 16.864,
"vo_start": 17.139,
"vo_end": 23.138,
"text": "At two dollars an hour, it came to 35 dollars. Knight's verdict: he didn't love it, but maybe it would grow on him.",
"end": 23.413
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 23.413,
"vo_start": 23.688,
"vo_end": 30.366,
"text": "The name came even closer to the deadline. Knight wanted 'Dimension Six'. Nobody else did.",
"end": 30.641
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 30.641,
"vo_start": 30.916,
"vo_end": 38.636,
"text": "Then Jeff Johnson said a word had come to him in a dream: Nike, the Greek goddess of victory. The boxes had to be printed, so Nike it was.",
"end": 38.911
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 38.911,
"vo_start": 39.186,
"vo_end": 46.666,
"text": "Davidson didn't stay a 35-dollar footnote. In 1983, Knight gave her a gold swoosh ring with a diamond, and Nike shares.",
"end": 46.941
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 46.941,
"vo_start": 47.216,
"vo_end": 56.397,
"text": "In 1972, the first Nike-branded shoes showed up at the US Olympic trials in Eugene, including a hand-made racer runners called the Moon Shoe.",
"end": 56.672
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 56.672,
"vo_start": 56.947,
"vo_end": 64.644,
"text": "Money was so tight that American banks kept refusing credit. A Japanese trading company, Nissho Iwai, kept the business afloat.",
"end": 64.919
},
{
"i": 9,
"start": 64.919,
"vo_start": 65.194,
"vo_end": 69.993,
"text": "The Onitsuka split went to court. In 1974, Blue Ribbon Sports won.",
"end": 70.943
}
],
